Verdicts on PID tuning rules for exothermic reactors depend on implementation details that are rarely reported, chiefly the anti-windup logic of a valve limited in amplitude and slew rate. A study of a three-state CSTR with eight PID tunings and seven integrator policies shows that sign-blind amplitude clamping, a frequent implementation error, latches a reverse-acting loop at full cooling (a proposition gives the offset equilibrium; here the integral absolute error of a $-2$~K step for Ziegler--Nichols rises from $0.940$ to $104.123$~K$\cdot$min). A slew-rate trigger restores the loop; over slew limits of $20$ to $320$~L/min$^2$ it matters for the empirical rules at every limit and for AMIGO and the optimal tuning when the valve is slow. Back-calculation with one tracking constant per loop, chosen by minimax over three steps, is within $2\%$ of the directional rule and up to $10\%$ better; common defaults are up to $97\%$ worse. Bounding $M_s \le 1.6$ on the exact linearization instead of the identified dead-time model lowers the optimal ITAE by $11\%$ in sample, with no gain out of sample and $76\%$ higher high-frequency gain. Noise-to-valve gain follows $|K_p|(1+N)$. On the feasible region, heat-removal demand explains only part of the error variance (Sobol and Shapley analyses).
